  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none mb-12">
    <h2>1. The Physics of Tributary Roof Loading</h2>
    <p>
      A garage door header does not simply support the wall above it; it carries a major proportion of the roof framing. The <strong>tributary width</strong> equals half the clear span of the roof trusses or ceiling joists resting on the exterior wall, plus any exterior roof overhang:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Formula:</strong> <code>Tributary Width = (Roof Span &divide; 2) + Overhang</code></li>
      <li>For a 24-foot wide garage with trusses spanning gable-to-gable, the tributary width on each bearing eave wall is <code>(24 &divide; 2) + 1.5 = 13.5 feet</code>.</li>
      <li>Every linear foot of your 16-foot garage door header supports 13.5 square feet of roof area. At 30 PSF snow load and 15 PSF dead load (45 PSF total load), the header carries <strong>607.5 pounds per linear foot</strong>, or nearly <strong>9,700 pounds</strong> of cumulative roof weight!</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. IRC Table R602.7(1) Sizing for Exterior Bearing Walls</h2>
    <p>
      For standard residential single-car garage openings (8-foot to 9-foot clear spans), the International Residential Code provides prescribed dimensional lumber sizing (assumes #2 Spruce-Pine-Fir or Douglas Fir-Larch):
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
      <table class="w-full text-sm text-left border border-slate-200 rounded-lg">
        <thead class="bg-slate-100 text-slate-800 font-bold border-b border-slate-200">
          <tr>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">Clear Span Opening</th>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">Roof Span &le; 24 Ft</th>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">Roof Span &le; 32 Ft</th>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">Jack Studs (Trimmers)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200 text-slate-700">
          <tr>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-slate-900">8 Feet (Single Car)</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-sky-700 font-bold">Double 2x10</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-sky-700 font-bold">Double 2x12</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">1 Jack Stud (1.5-in bearing)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-slate-900">9 Feet (Wide Single)</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-sky-700 font-bold">Double 2x12</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-sky-700 font-bold">Triple 2x10</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">1 Jack Stud (1.5-in bearing)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-slate-900">10 Feet (Max Dimensional)</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-sky-700 font-bold">Triple 2x12</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 text-red-600 font-bold">Exceeds Dimensional Wood</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold text-amber-700">2 Jack Studs (3.0-in bearing)</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>3. Why 16-Foot Double Garage Doors Require Engineered LVL</h2>
    <p>
      Standard 2x10s and 2x12s cannot physically span a 16-foot or 18-foot double-car garage door. Even if you laminated four 2x12s together, natural defects (knots, slope of grain, drying shrinkage) would cause the center of the beam to sag 1/2 to 3/4 inch under self-weight and live roof loads.
    </p>
    <p>
      Laminated Veneer Lumber (LVL) uses ultrasonic testing to align wood veneers, achieving an elasticity modulus of <strong>2.0E to 2.2E</strong> (compared to 1.4E for standard lumber) and allowable bending stress (Fb) of <strong>2,600 to 2,900 PSI</strong>.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
      <table class="w-full text-sm text-left border border-slate-200 rounded-lg">
        <thead class="bg-slate-100 text-slate-800 font-bold border-b border-slate-200">
          <tr>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">Clear Opening</th>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">Snow Load &le; 30 PSF</th>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">Heavy Snow (50 PSF)</th>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">Required Jack Studs</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200 text-slate-700">
          <tr>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-slate-900">16-Foot Double Door</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-sky-700 font-bold">(2) 1-3/4" &times; 11-7/8" LVL</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-emerald-700 font-bold">(3) 1-3/4" &times; 11-7/8" (or 2x 14")</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold text-sky-800">2 Jacks each side (3.0")</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-slate-900">18-Foot Wide Double</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-sky-700 font-bold">(2) 1-3/4" &times; 14" LVL</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-emerald-700 font-bold">(3) 1-3/4" &times; 14" LVL</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold text-sky-800">2 Jacks each side (3.0")</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>4. Deflection Limits: Why L/240 Is Not Enough for Garage Doors</h2>
    <p>
      Building codes often permit a total load deflection limit of <strong>L/240</strong> for general roof framing. However, for a 16-foot (192-inch) garage door opening:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>L/240 = 192 &divide; 240 = 0.80 inches (over 3/4 inch sag!)</code></li>
      <li>If a garage door header sags 0.80 inches, the upper weather seal binds, the top panel catches on the jamb, and the motorized opener trolley burns out its nylon drive gear.</li>
      <li>Engineers always design garage door headers to <strong>L/360 or L/480</strong> (maximum deflection under full load of 0.40 to 0.53 inches), ensuring buttery-smooth door operation even under peak winter snow loads.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

